<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ode to Joy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem written in the summer of <a href="/years/1785">1785</a>; Beethoven's setting in the Ninth Symphony completed in <a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as An die Freude</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a> wrote &quot;An die Freude&quot; in the summer of 1785. From the beginning of May until mid-September that year he stayed with his publisher, Georg Joachim Göschen, at Gohlis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, where he wrote the poem together with his play Don Carlos. The friendship of Christian Gottfried Körner, his longtime friend and patron, had inspired it. The ode was published the next year in the magazine Thalia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy.jpg" alt="Ode to Joy" width="330" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Schiller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schiller_an_die_freude_manuskript_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a> was a German poet, playwright and historian. In 1808 a slightly revised version of the ode, published after his death, changed two lines of the first stanza and left out the last stanza, which had asked for rescue from the chains of tyrants, mercy in the high courts and forgiveness for all sinners. This 1808 text forms the basis for Beethoven's setting.</p><p>Later in life Schiller regarded the poem as a failure. In an 1800 letter to Christian Gottfried Körner he called it detached from reality and of value perhaps for the two of them but not for the world. Scholars have speculated whether he first wrote an Ode to Freedom and changed it to Joy, but the musicologist Alexander Rehding points out that scholarly consensus holds there is no factual basis for this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The ode is a long poem in stanzas, each followed by a chorus. It addresses Joy as a daughter of Elysium, a power that binds together what custom has divided, so that all men become brothers. It praises friendship and love, calls on millions to embrace one another, and looks for a loving Father above the stars. It calls for courage in suffering and truth to friend and foe, and ends with a plea for pardon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The ode is best known for its use by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> in the final movement of his Ninth Symphony, completed in 1824. Beethoven's text is not based entirely on Schiller's poem and introduces a few new sections. Other composers set the poem too, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-schubert">Franz Schubert</a> in 1815, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a> in 1865 and Pietro Mascagni in 1882.</p><p>Beethoven's melody, but not Schiller's text, was adopted as the Anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> in 1972 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1985. It was also used for the national anthem of Rhodesia from 1974 until 1979. During the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> women sang a Spanish version outside prisons, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on Christmas Day 1989, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-bernstein">Leonard Bernstein</a> conducted it with choir and orchestra members from East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ode to Joy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the 1992 Summer Olympics in Barcelona the South African team used &quot;<b>Ode to Joy</b>&quot; and a neutral flag instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-stem-van-suid-afrika">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</a></li><li><blockquote>On 28 August 1974 the government announced its chosen music: the &quot;<b>Ode to Joy</b>&quot; from the fourth movement of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, which the Council of Europe had adopted as the Anthem of Europe in January 1972.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>Beethoven's Ninth, with its choral setting of Schiller's '<b>Ode to Joy</b>', premiered at the Kärntnertortheater.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
